PG.notes("s06-colors", "<p class=\"summary\">Opens the CSS properties section with colour: background-color versus color, named colours, and hex codes (with a pointer to Color Hunt for palettes). Ends with a five-step exercise colouring a page with named colours and hex codes.</p>\n<h3>Property and value recap</h3>\n<ul><li>A CSS declaration is property, colon, value. The property says what to change; the value says what to set it to.</li><li>background-color fills the area behind an element. Applied to body (the html content wrapper in practice), it colours the whole page.</li><li>color sets the colour of the text inside the element, not its background. Check MDN when unsure which property controls what.</li><li>background-color works on any element: it fills the element's own box (e.g. an h1's block area), not just the body.</li></ul>\n<h3>Named colours</h3>\n<ul><li>CSS defines a built-in list of colour keywords such as cornflowerblue, cadetblue, dimgrey, olivedrab, antiquewhite, whitesmoke, darkseagreen.</li><li>The full list is on MDN (search 'MDN named color'). The table shows each name next to its hex code and a swatch.</li><li>The 'CSS Level 1/2/3' labels in that table only record when each name was added historically; ignore them when choosing.</li><li>Usage: copy the keyword and paste it as the value, no quotes, no hash.</li></ul>\n<h3>Hex codes and custom palettes</h3>\n<ul><li>Named colours are limited, so for a custom look use a palette tool. The lecture recommends colorhunt.co: free designer-made palettes of several colours (background tone, heading tone, accent tones).</li><li>Palette tools give hex codes like #5D3891 rather than names. A hex code is a compact way of writing an RGB colour.</li><li>RGB: a colour is a mix of red, green and blue, each from 0 to 255. The example purple is 93 red, 56 green, 145 blue; its hex form is the same three numbers written in base 16.</li><li>Hex format: # followed by six hex digits, two each for red, green, blue (#RRGGBB).</li><li>In code a hex value goes exactly where a name would: color: #faf8f1;</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Hex digits run 0-9 then a-f, so 00 is none and ff is full (255). #000000 is black, #ffffff is white. Also valid: three-digit shorthand like #fff, rgb(), hsl().</li><li>Pick colours that work as a set: one for the background, one for headings, one for subtitles, plus accents.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Two properties, two kinds of value</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">body {\n  background-color: antiquewhite; /* named colour */\n}\n\nh2 {\n  color: #faf8f1;            /* text colour, hex */\n  background-color: #c58940; /* box fill, hex */\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: 6.1 CSS Colors</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> index.html has a page with just an h1 and an h2 (Hello / World) and no colour; the preview is black text on white.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Add CSS so the page matches goal.png: a coloured page background, a coloured h1 with a background, and a coloured h2 with a background. The h1 must use named colours; the h2 must use hex codes, to practise both notations.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Set the body background-color to antiquewhite.</li><li>Set the h1 text colour (color) to whitesmoke.</li><li>Set the h1 background-color to darkseagreen.</li><li>Set the h2 text colour to the hex value #faf8f1.</li><li>Set the h2 background-color to the hex value #c58940. Save and check the preview against the goal image.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Antique-white page; h1 pale text on a sage-green band; h2 cream text on a tan/gold band.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">body {\n  background-color: antiquewhite;\n}\n\nh1 {\n  color: whitesmoke;\n  background-color: darkseagreen;\n}\n\nh2 {\n  color: #faf8f1;\n  background-color: #c58940;\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using color when you meant background-color (or the reverse): color only affects text.</li><li>Forgetting the # before a hex code, which makes the declaration invalid and ignored.</li><li>Needing 'lateral thinking': background-color is allowed on h1/h2 too, not only on body.</li><li>Reference repo note: it carries an author footer ('Made with...') that is not part of the exercise; ignore it.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s06-fonts", "<p class=\"summary\">Covers the main text-styling properties: font-size (px, pt, em, rem, keywords), font-weight, font-family with generic fallbacks and Google Fonts, and text-align. Finishes with a TODO-driven exercise using an id per paragraph.</p>\n<h3>font-size and its units</h3>\n<ul><li>font-size sets text size. Common absolute units: px (1/96 inch, about 0.26 mm) and pt (1/72 inch, about 0.35 mm, a little larger than a px).</li><li>Points are the unit word processors use, so 12pt on a page at 100% zoom looks roughly like 12 in Word.</li><li>em is relative to the parent element's font size: with a parent at 20px, 1em = 20px and 2em = 40px. Change the parent to 80px and the child becomes 160px, so the value moves with whatever the parent is.</li><li>rem ('root em') is relative to the root element (the html element). 1rem = root size, 2rem = twice root size. Changing a nearby parent such as a footer has no effect; only changing the html font-size does.</li><li>Recommendation: prefer rem. With nested elements (h2 in footer in body in html) em becomes confusing, especially across separate CSS files, while rem always points to one place.</li><li>Keyword sizes also exist (e.g. large, x-large, xx-large). px and pt are fixed; em and rem are relative.</li><li>The lesson's font-size.html demo lets you change the root, the em/rem values and keywords to see them respond; lowering the html size from xx-large to 20px shrinks everything declared in rem.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Browser default root size is 16px, so 1rem = 16px unless overridden.</li></ul>\n<h3>font-weight</h3>\n<ul><li>Keywords: normal and bold.</li><li>Relative keywords: lighter and bolder, which adjust the weight relative to the parent's weight.</li><li>Numeric: 100 to 900 in steps of 100 (400 = normal, 700 = bold, 900 = heaviest). Whether you see each step depends on the font having that weight.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The numeric scale is the same one Google Fonts uses when it lists weights such as 400 and 700.</li></ul>\n<h3>font-family and fallbacks</h3>\n<ul><li>font-family picks the typeface. Helvetica is installed on Macs but not many Windows machines, so always list one or more fallbacks ending with a generic family.</li><li>Syntax: comma-separated list, tried left to right. The first available font wins. Example: Helvetica, sans-serif.</li><li>Font names that contain spaces must be in quotation marks, e.g. &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif.</li><li>Serif fonts have small decorative strokes ('feet') at letter ends; sans-serif fonts do not. Match the generic fallback to the primary font's kind.</li><li>Other generic families: cursive, monospace (every character equal width, like code or a typewriter), fantasy. The actual font behind each generic differs by OS and browser (sans-serif is often Arial-like on Windows).</li><li>Typography is covered in more depth in later lessons.</li></ul>\n<h3>Adding a Google Font</h3>\n<ul><li>Browse fonts.google.com and open a font. Some families offer several weights, others only regular (400).</li><li>Select the style(s) you want (e.g. regular 400, bold 700); the selections panel in the top right lists them and lets you remove entries.</li><li>Choose the &lt;link&gt; option and copy it. Paste it inside the head element, outside the style element (placement matters).</li><li>Copy the CSS rule Google gives for that family and put it in your stylesheet on the element you want, e.g. the h1.</li><li>The link downloads the font for every visitor, regardless of what is installed on their computer. The fallback generic family in the CSS (e.g. cursive) is used if the link fails.</li></ul>\n<h3>text-align</h3>\n<ul><li>Values include left, right, center, plus start and end.</li><li>start/end follow writing direction: for left-to-right Western text start is the left, but for right-to-left scripts such as Arabic start is the right.</li><li>Check MDN for further values (e.g. justify) and the interactive demo.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">em versus rem</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">html   { font-size: 30px; }\nfooter { font-size: 80px; }\n\n/* inside the footer: */\nh2.em  { font-size: 2em;  } /* 2 x footer = 160px */\nh2.rem { font-size: 2rem; } /* 2 x html   = 60px, footer ignored */</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Font stacks with fallbacks</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">p.a { font-family: Helvetica, sans-serif; }\np.b { font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif; }   /* quotes: name has spaces */\np.c { font-family: &quot;Caveat&quot;, cursive; }          /* Google Font, generic backup */</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Font weight options</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">h1 { font-weight: bold; }\np  { font-weight: 300; }\nspan { font-weight: bolder; } /* relative to parent */</pre>\n<h2>The task: 6.2 Font Properties: TODO list</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> index.html has a body (cornflowerblue background, white text, 18px) and a heading plus five paragraphs with ids color, size, weight, family, align. A comment says not to alter the existing CSS but add yours below it in the same style element. goal.png shows the target. Separate font-size.html and font-family.html are the demos.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Complete each TODO by adding rules below the existing CSS, keeping the Google Font link outside the style element.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Select #color and set its text colour to the named colour coral.</li><li>Select #size and set font-size to 2rem (twice the root size).</li><li>Select #weight and set font-weight to 900.</li><li>Choose Caveat (regular weight) on Google Fonts, paste its &lt;link&gt; tags in the head just before the head closes and outside the style element, then give #family the font-family rule with a backup generic (cursive).</li><li>Select #align and set text-align to right.</li><li>Select the html element and set font-size to 30px; the 2rem paragraph should grow to match (60px).</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A blue page: coral Color line, a large Font Size line that scales with the root, a very bold Font Weight line, a handwriting-style Font Family line, and a right-aligned Text Align line.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">#color  { color: coral; }\n#size   { font-size: 2rem; }\n#weight { font-weight: 900; }\n#align  { text-align: right; }\n#family {\n  font-family: &quot;Caveat&quot;, cursive;\n  font-weight: 400;\n}\nhtml { font-size: 30px; }\n\n/* in &lt;head&gt;, outside &lt;style&gt;: */\n/* &lt;link rel=&quot;preconnect&quot; href=&quot;https://fonts.googleapis.com&quot; /&gt;\n   &lt;link rel=&quot;preconnect&quot; href=&quot;https://fonts.gstatic.com&quot; crossorigin /&gt;\n   &lt;link href=&quot;https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Caveat&amp;display=swap&quot; rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; /&gt; */</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Putting the Google Fonts link inside the style element; it belongs in the head as its own link element.</li><li>Forgetting quotes around multi-word font names.</li><li>Omitting a generic fallback so users without the font see an arbitrary default.</li><li>Mixing em values inside nested elements and losing track of which parent applies.</li><li>Reference repo adds font-optical-sizing and font-style lines copied from Google's snippet, plus an author footer; neither is required.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l040", "<p class=\"summary\">A tour of Chrome DevTools for CSS: opening the inspector, the Elements and Styles panes, live editing, overridden (struck-out) rules, the Computed tab and CSS Overview. Ends with a four-question quiz on a practice site where answers are found with the inspector.</p>\n<h3>Opening DevTools</h3>\n<ul><li>Chrome only (the course assumes Chrome so everyone sees the same tools). Menu: three-dots, More tools, Developer tools.</li><li>Shortcuts: Cmd+Option+I on Mac, Ctrl+Shift+I on Windows, or F12 on many keyboards. The menu shows the exact shortcut for your machine.</li><li>Right-click any element and choose Inspect: opens DevTools and selects that element in the HTML.</li><li>The element-picker (arrow icon at the top-left of DevTools) lets you click an item on the page and jump to its HTML.</li></ul>\n<h3>Elements tab and Styles pane</h3>\n<ul><li>The Elements tab shows the live DOM. The Styles pane beside it lists every CSS rule that applies to the selected element.</li><li>Browsers give every element default styles (the user-agent stylesheet). Turning the site's CSS off shows only these defaults.</li><li>Your own rules appear above the defaults, labelled with the file and line they came from (e.g. styles.css); clicking the label opens that source.</li><li>When two rules set the same property, the later/more specific one wins and the loser is shown struck through. Example: an anchor's default blue colour is crossed out because the site sets white. Unticking the site rule re-activates the default.</li></ul>\n<h3>Live editing is temporary</h3>\n<ul><li>Use the + button in the Styles pane to add a declaration to the selected element, e.g. color: black on an h1; the page updates instantly.</li><li>Each declaration has a checkbox (or crossing control) to toggle it off and on to compare.</li><li>Nothing you do in DevTools changes the source files or the real site. It only changes the copy loaded in your browser and disappears on reload. Editing a big site's text this way does not alter that site or other users' views.</li><li>Workflow: experiment in DevTools, then copy the rules you like into your own stylesheet.</li><li>Confirm by opening the file in the Sources tab: the new rule is not there.</li></ul>\n<h3>Computed tab</h3>\n<ul><li>Computed shows the final resolved values for the element in one flat list, hiding crossed-out noise. For example the link colour appears as an rgb(red, green, blue) value.</li><li>Useful for inherited properties: a paragraph's font-family may not be set on the p at all but inherited from body or html, and Computed still shows the value in use.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> In the Styles pane, inherited rules appear under an 'Inherited from ...' heading.</li></ul>\n<h3>CSS Overview</h3>\n<ul><li>Open the DevTools own three-dots menu (not Chrome's), More tools, CSS Overview, then press Capture overview.</li><li>It summarises the page's background colours, text colours, fonts (font families in use) and more (media queries and unused declarations are skipped for now).</li><li>Handy for studying sites you like: find which colours and fonts they use and copy the values.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Editing a rule in the Styles pane (temporary, browser-only)</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">element.style {\n  color: black; /* added with the + button; vanishes on reload */\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: CSS inspection quiz</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Open the practice site at appbrewery.github.io/css-inspection/ in Chrome. It has an h1, an h2 and a paragraph, plus four answer fields and a Submit button.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Use DevTools to find four CSS values and submit them to see if each is correct.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Inspect the body and read its background colour (named colour).</li><li>Inspect the h1 and read its font-size.</li><li>Inspect the h2 and read its font-weight.</li><li>Inspect the paragraph and find its font-family. It is inherited, so look in the inherited section of Styles or switch to the Computed tab.</li><li>Type each answer into its field and press Submit.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> All four answers marked correct.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"txt\">body background-color: aliceblue\nh1 font-size: 3rem\nh2 font-weight: 500\np font-family: Arial, sans-serif (inherited; visible in Computed)</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Expecting DevTools edits to be saved; they are lost on reload and never touch the real file.</li><li>Confusing Chrome's three-dots menu with DevTools' own three-dots menu when looking for CSS Overview.</li><li>Looking only at the paragraph's own rules and missing a font-family that is inherited.</li><li>Reading crossed-out declarations as active; the struck-through one is overridden.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s06-box-model", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains the box model: every element is a rectangular box with content (width/height), padding, border and margin. Covers border shorthand, one-to-four value syntax, the div container and the Pesticide extension, then an exercise placing three boxes corner to corner.</p>\n<h3>Every element is a box</h3>\n<ul><li>Each HTML element is laid out as an invisible rectangle. height and width set the size of its content area; making a box taller pushes later elements further down the page.</li><li>width and height accept px or percentages. A width of 100% fills the parent's width.</li><li>From the inside out the layers are: content (width x height), padding, border, margin.</li></ul>\n<h3>border</h3>\n<ul><li>Shorthand takes three values separated by spaces: thickness, style, colour. Example: border: 10px solid black.</li><li>Style can be solid, dashed and others; colour is any named or hex colour.</li><li>In this model the border sits outside the declared width/height. A bigger border does not shrink the content box; it adds to the element's overall size.</li><li>Fine-tune afterwards with border-width. It must come after the shorthand line, since later declarations override earlier ones. Example: border: 30px solid black; then border-top-width: 0 removes just the top.</li><li>border-width accepts one to four values (see multi-value syntax below).</li></ul>\n<h3>padding versus margin</h3>\n<ul><li>padding is the gap between the content and the border, inside the border. The element's background colour covers the padding. Adding padding pushes the border outwards; the declared width/height stays the same.</li><li>margin is the gap outside the border, separating the element from its neighbours. Margin is transparent.</li><li>Two adjacent boxes each with margin 10px appear 20px apart in the lecture's example (10 from each side).</li><li>Padding adds space inside the visual box; margin adds space between boxes.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Vertical margins between stacked block elements can collapse to the larger of the two instead of adding, so the 20px figure applies to the horizontal case.</li></ul>\n<h3>One to four values (padding, margin, border-width)</h3>\n<ul><li>Four values go clockwise from the top: top, right, bottom, left.</li><li>Two values: first is top and bottom, second is left and right (e.g. 0 20px sets vertical 0, horizontal 20px).</li><li>One value applies to all four sides.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Three values: top, left and right, bottom.</li><li>Single-side properties also exist: margin-left, padding-top, border-top-width and so on.</li></ul>\n<h3>Inspecting the box model</h3>\n<ul><li>DevTools shows a nested diagram (margin, border, padding, content) with colours for each layer and the numbers editable.</li><li>Practice site: appbrewery.github.io/box-model/. Select an image, then in the Styles tab's box diagram type a margin of 100px to see gaps appear above/below, or padding of 100px to see space inside the border. Adding a 10px solid black border shows how padding sits inside it.</li></ul>\n<h3>The div element and Pesticide</h3>\n<ul><li>div (content division) is a generic invisible container. Put any number of elements between its tags to group them so they can be styled or positioned together, e.g. an image with its caption.</li><li>A div has no look of its own, which is why it is taught only now that you can style boxes. It also adds structure to the HTML.</li><li>Pesticide is a free Chrome extension that outlines every element and div, making box model problems visible. Hold Ctrl and hover over a box to see its tag, classes and id in the bottom bar.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Border shorthand then override</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">p {\n  border: 30px solid black;  /* width style colour, all sides */\n  border-top-width: 0;       /* must come after the shorthand */\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Multi-value syntax</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.a { margin: 10px; }                 /* all sides */\n.b { margin: 10px 40px; }            /* top+bottom | left+right */\n.c { margin: 0 10px 20px 30px; }     /* top | right | bottom | left */</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">A div grouping a caption and an image</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;div&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;Morning view&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;img src=&quot;morning.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Earth by day&quot; /&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: 6.3 Box model: three boxes corner to corner</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> index.html contains a list of TODO comments for you to follow like client instructions; goal.png shows the result, annotated with 20px padding, a 20px top/bottom and 10px left/right border on one box, and boxes touching corner to corner. The ref repo's index already holds the finished solution.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Build three divs (ids first, second, third) of the same size, each with different padding, border and margin, so they touch diagonally at their corners.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Add three divs to the body with ids first, second and third; put a paragraph of text in the first so padding is visible.</li><li>Give all divs height: 200px and width: 200px.</li><li>first: cadetblue background, padding 20px, border 10px solid black.</li><li>Reset the paragraph's margin to 0. Browsers give p a default margin of 1em top and bottom, which would push it off the div's edge.</li><li>second: gold background, solid black border with border-width of 20px top/bottom and 10px left/right (two-value form).</li><li>Work out the margin so second touches the bottom-right corner of first: first's outer width = 200 + 20 + 20 padding + 10 + 10 border = 260px, so margin-left: 260px.</li><li>third: indianred background, border 10px solid black, margin-left so its top-right corner meets second's bottom-left corner (second's left edge is at 260px and the box is 220px wide, giving 40px).</li><li>Check against the goal image; trial and error with DevTools works too.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A staircase of three coloured boxes, each touching the next exactly at a corner with no gaps or overlap.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">div {\n  height: 200px;\n  width: 200px;\n}\n\n#first {\n  background-color: cadetblue;\n  padding: 20px;\n  border: 10px solid black;\n}\n\n#second {\n  background-color: gold;\n  border: solid black;\n  border-width: 20px 10px;\n  margin-left: 260px;\n}\n\n#third {\n  background-color: indianred;\n  border: 10px solid black;\n  margin-left: 40px;\n}\n\np {\n  margin: 0;\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting the browser's default margin on p, which shifts text away from the div's padding.</li><li>Placing border-width before the border shorthand, which then overwrites it.</li><li>Forgetting that padding and border add to the rendered size when computing offsets (260px, not 200px).</li><li>Mixing up the order of four values: it is clockwise from top, not alphabetical.</li><li>Reference repo has an author footer element that is not in the exercise.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s06-meme", "<p class=\"summary\">Section project: build a motivational-poster page with a black background, a white-bordered image, an uppercase title in a Google font and a caption, all centred in a poster div. The walkthrough shows the small HTML and CSS solution.</p>\n<h3>Project overview</h3>\n<ul><li>Target look: a 1990s-style poster. Large image with a border, black page, big title above or below, short paragraph of text, all centred.</li><li>Consolidates the section: colours, borders, fonts, text-align, margins, box model and the div container.</li><li>The starter folder includes an assets/images folder with a sample photo; using your own image is recommended for practice. The task list has six points to satisfy; afterwards personalise freely.</li></ul>\n<h3>Techniques the brief points to</h3>\n<ul><li>Wrap the image, h1 and paragraph in one div so a single box can be centred and styled instead of three separate elements.</li><li>Centre horizontally with a percentage width and margin: width 50% leaves 50% of the screen free, so a left margin of 25% pushes the box to the middle. (The video says 20% in passing; 25% is the arithmetic that actually centres.)</li><li>Set the image width to 100% so it fills the poster div and needs no separate centring.</li><li>Vertical position: a rough margin-top in pixels judged by eye. Proper centring techniques come in later sections.</li><li>Use a Google Font (Libre Baskerville) imitating the poster title typeface; link it in the head (as with the earlier font lesson) and reference it with a serif fallback.</li><li>Image border: 5px solid white.</li><li>Make the title uppercase using text-transform rather than retyping it; the brief asks you to find this property in the docs yourself.</li><li>Use a class (not an id) on the poster div so the style could be reused if the site grows.</li></ul>\n<h3>How the solution works</h3>\n<ul><li>body: black background-color.</li><li>.poster: white text colour, black background, text-align center, width 50%, margin-left 25%, margin-top 100px, font-family Libre Baskerville with serif fallback. Children inherit the colour, centring and font.</li><li>.motivation-img: border 5px solid white; width 100% so it fills the poster.</li><li>h1: text-transform uppercase and a larger font-size (3rem).</li><li>The HTML head contains the stylesheet link plus the Google Fonts link tags. The body contains one div.poster with an img, an h1 and a p.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution HTML (body and head essentials)</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;./style.css&quot; /&gt;\n  &lt;link rel=&quot;preconnect&quot; href=&quot;https://fonts.googleapis.com&quot; /&gt;\n  &lt;link rel=&quot;preconnect&quot; href=&quot;https://fonts.gstatic.com&quot; crossorigin /&gt;\n  &lt;link href=&quot;https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Libre+Baskerville&amp;display=swap&quot; rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; /&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;div class=&quot;poster&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;img class=&quot;motivation-img&quot; src=&quot;./assets/images/daenerys.jpeg&quot; alt=&quot;daenerys with egg&quot; /&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;That Special Moment&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;When you find the perfect avocado at the supermarket&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: 6.4 Motivation Meme Project</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Starter folder with index.html, style.css, an assets/images folder (sample image) and goal.png. The brief is a six-point TODO list; the finished version is in a solution folder.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Reproduce the poster in goal.png with your own image and text, using the properties from this section.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Add the Libre Baskerville Google Font link(s) to the head.</li><li>Put your image in assets/images and reference it with an img element (with alt text).</li><li>Wrap the image, title and paragraph in a div with a class such as poster.</li><li>Set a black page background and white text.</li><li>Give the image a 5px solid white border and width 100%.</li><li>Centre the text, give the div width 50% and margin-left 25% to centre it horizontally, and add a margin-top (about 100px) to move it down.</li><li>Apply the Libre Baskerville font (with serif backup) to the div.</li><li>Make the h1 appear uppercase with text-transform.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A centred black poster: white-bordered picture filling a half-width column, an uppercase serif title and a caption beneath, in white.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">body {\n  background-color: black;\n}\n\n.poster {\n  color: white;\n  background-color: black;\n  text-align: center;\n  width: 50%;\n  margin-left: 25%;\n  margin-top: 100px;\n  font-family: &quot;Libre Baskerville&quot;, serif;\n}\n\n.motivation-img {\n  border: 5px solid white;\n  width: 100%;\n}\n\nh1 {\n  text-transform: uppercase;\n  font-size: 3rem;\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting to link the Google Font in the head, so the serif fallback shows.</li><li>Using 20% instead of 25% left margin, which leaves the poster off-centre.</li><li>Typing the title in capitals instead of using text-transform.</li><li>Not wrapping the elements in a div, then having to centre three elements separately.</li><li>Reference repo uses a different sample picture and caption plus an author footer; the footer is not part of the brief.</li></ul>");
